### CVE-2020-26760

#### Details

A vulnerability has been discovered in ext4 where directory traversal can result in access to files outside the directory in which the file name is located, even for regular files, and in the case of hard links, directories that contain symbolic links can be exploited to gain access to other arbitrary files or directories. This can result in information disclosure or arbitrary file access. The vulnerability is present in the EXT4 filesystem in the check_dir function, which does not properly handle the '..' part in a directory name component (see the code below). The '..' part can lead to an overflow in the destination directory pathname and overwrite the return value from snprintf which is then used for calculating the final destination directory. If this vulnerability is exploited correctly it can lead to access to files outside the directory of the file where the vulnerability exists, and information disclosure.  Also it leads to possible access to other filesystem entries (both directories and files) which then can be exploited. This is possible because the directory names are being generated in the stack memory, and later passed into the ext4_expand_dir_name function, which may expand them. This function expands directory component names to full path names using open files, and the paths can be symlinks.  In the check_dir function the initial file name is added to the stack path, and the destination directory path is then expanded during the check for '..'.  In this case '..' may point to components of the stack path in order to find an earlier part of the directory hierarchy in which the file name is located. An attacker can send a specially crafted file name to trigger this vulnerability. 

